  title = {Kant and the Supposed Right to Lie},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.1017/9781108992435},
  note = {SUMMARY: A monograph on Kant's 1797 essay On a Supposed Right to Lie from Love of Humanity, which examines its background (the provocation by Benjamin Constant and his translator Carl Friedrich Cramer), the history of the murderer-at-the-door example (Augustine, Fichte, Michaelis), Constant's version of the case, and Kant's core argument that lying, or a right to lie, would undermine contractual rights and spell disaster for all humanity. It includes a facing-page translation of the essay (publisher abstract).

