  title = {Kant Incorporated},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.1017/9781009641364},
  note = {SUMMARY: A Cambridge Element arguing that Kant's practical philosophy offers a systematic basis for understanding corporations, such as universities, churches, feudal orders and early overseas business corporations. Corporations bridge the distinctions between ethics and right and between public and private right, and can extend freedom, structure moral activity and aid progress, but the unequal liability of those who exercise corporate powers threatens Kant's principle of innate equality; the Element explores the safeguards needed (publisher abstract).

